# LINE ENDINGS ARE PART OF THE PRODUCT CONTRACT, NOT A PREFERENCE.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true by default, so without this
# file a Windows team's clone converts every shipped text file to CRLF. Two
# independent failures follow, both measured 2026-09-03:
#
#   1. `#!/usr/bin/env bash` gains a CR. The installer does not run at all --
#      "$'\r': command not found", "set: pipefail^M: invalid option name",
#      exit 2, which is not even a documented exit code.
#   2. The installer validates the settings template against a SHA-256 baked
#      into the script. CRLF changes the bytes, so the hash misses and the run
#      dies with exit 14, "baseline source files missing or unreadable" -- a
#      message that is false. The file is present and readable.
#
# Both are fixed by checking these files out with LF on every platform. Git Bash
# reads LF correctly on Windows; it is the CR that breaks things.
#
# Do NOT relax this to `text=auto`. The line-ending regression test in the build repository
# exists for exactly this, and it clones the published distribution to check it.

* text=auto eol=lf

# BINARY FILES MUST BE DECLARED, OR THE RULE ABOVE CORRUPTS THEM. `text=auto` asks git to
# guess, and a PNG whose bytes happen to look text-ish gets line-ending conversion applied
# to its pixel data -- the file is then a corrupt image on the clone, with no error anywhere.
# Measured 2026-09-05: adding docs/images/*.png without this stanza produced a corrupted
# PNG in a Windows-style clone of the published export, caught by the line-ending
# regression test in the build repository. Declare every binary extension the distribution ships.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.ico  binary

# The verdict contract and every shipped script are byte-compared somewhere.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.txt  text eol=lf
*.template text eol=lf
